  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The <b>\"Create an IAM Role and configure the EC2 instances to assume this role, granting permissions to access the S3 bucket\"</b> option is the best security practice. Assigning roles to EC2 provides temporary security credentials managed by AWS, eliminating the need to store long-term credentials on the instance and minimizing the risk of exposure.</p><p>Let's review why the other options are <i>not</i> ideal:</p><ul><li><i>\"Set up an IAM user and provide the EC2 instances with that user's access keys for bucket access.\"</i> – This method asks you to store static credentials on EC2, which is insecure and difficult to manage at scale.</li><li><i>\"Implement S3 cross-region replication for object integrity and security.\"</i> – Cross-region replication ensures resilience and geographic redundancy, but it does not address IAM security or credential management between instances and S3.</li><li><i>\"Enable versioning on the S3 bucket objects and activate MFA Delete for added control.\"</i> – These features strengthen object-level protection and accidental deletion, but do not solve credential management or instance authorization to access the bucket.</li></ul><p><b>Tip:</b> Always favor IAM Roles over IAM users for AWS service-to-service communication, as roles provide limited, managed, and temporary permissions, keeping credentials safer.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your organization runs EC2 instances that retrieve data from an S3 bucket. The IT Security team would like you to enhance the security of this setup. Which solution properly accomplishes this?</p>",
